CORE
main.cpp
--cpp17 --show-goto-functions
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^.*std::optional<signed_int>::optional\(this,rref_signed_int\).*$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^PARSING ERROR$
--
C++17 std::optional direct-initialization from a value
(`std::optional<int> o(42);`) with has_value()/operator*.

This verifies the frontend selects `std::optional`'s converting
constructor `optional(_Up&&)` and constructs the contained value.

Verified at the goto-program level rather than via full BMC: a
correctly-elaborated `std::optional<int>` pulls in the entire
`_Optional_base`/`_Optional_payload` instantiation graph, which
exceeds symbolic-execution memory limits.  (Before the
function-template converting-constructor deduction was fixed, this
construction silently failed and `main` was elided to a single
nondet return — the BMC "SUCCESSFUL" was vacuous, verifying
nothing.)  The goto-level check confirms the converting
constructor is actually invoked with the `int&&` value.
